100 DAYS OF PYTHON
DAY 33 — MATPLOTLIB & DATA VISUALIZATION

==================================================
1. DATA VISUALIZATION
==================================================

Data visualization means representing data using graphical
elements such as charts and plots.

It helps to:
- Identify trends and patterns
- Compare values
- Understand distributions
- Find relationships between variables
- Present data clearly

Common applications:
- Sales analysis
- Business reports
- Customer analysis
- Stock market analysis
- Scientific research
- Machine learning


==================================================
2. MATPLOTLIB
==================================================

Matplotlib is a Python library used to create different
types of graphs and charts.

Basic import:

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

Basic plotting structure:

plt.plot(x, y)
plt.show()


==================================================
3. LINE PLOT
==================================================

A line plot connects data points with lines.

Useful for:
- Trends over time
- Continuous data
- Comparing changes

Syntax:

plt.plot(x, y)

Common parameters:
- color
- marker
- linestyle
- linewidth
- alpha
- label

Example:

months = ["Jan", "Feb", "Mar"]
sales = [100, 150, 130]

plt.plot(months, sales, marker="o")
plt.title("Monthly Sales")
plt.xlabel("Month")
plt.ylabel("Sales")
plt.show()


==================================================
4. BAR CHART
==================================================

A bar chart represents values using rectangular bars.

Useful for:
- Comparing categories
- Department-wise values
- Product comparisons

Syntax:

plt.bar(x, y)

Common parameters:
- color
- width
- edgecolor
- alpha
- label

Example:

departments = ["CSE", "ECE", "EEE"]
students = [60, 45, 35]

plt.bar(departments, students)
plt.title("Department Strength")
plt.xlabel("Department")
plt.ylabel("Students")
plt.show()


==================================================
5. HISTOGRAM
==================================================

A histogram displays the frequency distribution of numerical
data.

The data is divided into intervals called bins.

Useful for:
- Exam scores
- Age distribution
- Salary distribution
- Data distribution analysis

Syntax:

plt.hist(data)

Important parameters:
- bins
- color
- edgecolor
- alpha
- histtype
- density

Example:

scores = [45, 50, 55, 60, 65, 70, 72, 80, 85, 90]

plt.hist(scores, bins=5, edgecolor="black")
plt.title("Score Distribution")
plt.xlabel("Score")
plt.ylabel("Frequency")
plt.show()


==================================================
6. SCATTER PLOT
==================================================

A scatter plot displays individual data points.

Useful for identifying relationships between two variables.

Example:
Study hours and examination marks.

Syntax:

plt.scatter(x, y)

Common parameters:
- s
- c
- cmap
- alpha
- marker
- edgecolors
- linewidths

Example:

hours = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
marks = [45, 50, 60, 70, 85]

plt.scatter(hours, marks)
plt.title("Study Hours vs Marks")
plt.xlabel("Study Hours")
plt.ylabel("Marks")
plt.show()


==================================================
7. PIE CHART
==================================================

A pie chart represents parts of a whole using sectors.

Useful for:
- Market share
- Budget distribution
- Category percentages

Syntax:

plt.pie(values, labels=labels)

Important parameters:
- labels
- autopct
- startangle
- explode
- shadow
- colors

Example:

values = [40, 30, 20, 10]
labels = ["A", "B", "C", "D"]

plt.pie(values, labels=labels, autopct="%1.1f%%")
plt.title("Market Share")
plt.show()


==================================================
8. BOX PLOT
==================================================

A box plot summarizes the distribution of numerical data.

It can help identify:
- Median
- Quartiles
- Spread
- Outliers

Syntax:

plt.boxplot(data)

Useful parameters:
- labels
- vert
- notch
- patch_artist
- showmeans
- meanline
- boxprops
- medianprops
- flierprops

Example:

salary = [25000, 28000, 30000, 32000, 35000, 50000]

plt.boxplot(salary)
plt.title("Salary Distribution")
plt.ylabel("Salary")
plt.show()


==================================================
9. AREA CHART
==================================================

An area chart is similar to a line plot but fills the region
below the line.

Useful for:
- Showing trends
- Cumulative values
- Website visitors
- Sales over time

Syntax:

plt.fill_between(x, y)

Example:

months = ["Jan", "Feb", "Mar", "Apr"]
visitors = [100, 150, 180, 220]

plt.fill_between(months, visitors, alpha=0.5)
plt.title("Website Visitors")
plt.xlabel("Month")
plt.ylabel("Visitors")
plt.show()


==================================================
10. HEATMAP
==================================================

A heatmap represents numerical values using different color
intensities.

Seaborn is commonly used to create heatmaps.

Import:

import seaborn as sns

Syntax:

sns.heatmap(data)

Useful parameters:
- annot
- fmt
- cmap
- linewidths
- xticklabels
- yticklabels
- cbar
- square

Example:

import seaborn as sns
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

data = [
    [80, 75, 90],
    [70, 85, 88],
    [92, 80, 95]
]

sns.heatmap(data, annot=True)
plt.title("Student Marks")
plt.show()


==================================================
11. SUBPLOTS
==================================================

Subplots allow multiple graphs to be displayed in one figure.

Syntax:

plt.subplot(rows, columns, position)

Example:

plt.subplot(1, 2, 1)
plt.plot([1, 2, 3], [10, 20, 30])

plt.subplot(1, 2, 2)
plt.bar(["A", "B", "C"], [20, 40, 30])

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()


==================================================
12. FIGURE CUSTOMIZATION
==================================================

Common functions used to improve charts:

plt.title()
    Adds a title.

plt.xlabel()
    Adds a label to the X-axis.

plt.ylabel()
    Adds a label to the Y-axis.

plt.legend()
    Displays labels for plotted data.

plt.grid()
    Adds grid lines.

plt.show()
    Displays the graph.

plt.tight_layout()
    Adjusts spacing between subplots.

plt.suptitle()
    Adds an overall title to a figure.


